            "anchor": "press"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "origin_par",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Shrujan’s journey began in 1969 when Kutch was ravaged by drought. Mumbai-based Chanda Shroff volunteered for drought-relief work in the village of Dhaneti. She observed that the women dressed in exquisitely embroidered traditional clothing were reluctant to accept charity."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "origin_par",
          "settings": {
            "text": "It was her genius that recognized that the very skill that the craftswomen possessed could empower them; that, for the first time, embroidery could become an income-generating, dignity-providing resource."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "origin_par",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Shrujan’s craftswomen work from home. Our production team reaches fabrics and threads to them in their villages. The women do not have to pay for the materials. Shrujan pays them their fair dues at their doorstep, as soon as they have completed the embroidery."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "origin_shot",
          "settings": {
            "asset": "story-origin-2.webp",
            "alt": "Craftswomen of Kutch at work on embroidery"
